<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;this&nbsp;for&nbsp;line-by-line&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not finding any serious issues with the containment section, so good job!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2375 is a necrotic skin condition in its beginning stages</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I suggest moving the &quot;in its beginning stages&quot; to the beginning of the sentence. I just think it reads a little clearer that way.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a necrotic skin condition</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Bacterial? Viral? Fungal? Might want to specify.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>appearing to suffer only physical decay</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Might want to add something like &quot;of the skin&quot; in there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the individual's limbs (if available)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure that the qualifier is really necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Access Granted. Welcome, Site Director.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't a problem, in and of itself, but would this info really only be restricted to Directors? I'd imagine they don't actually do a lot of the experimenting themselves.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>With the approval of five members of the O5 Council</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, that seems like a major overestimate of security needs.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>before audio feel cuts out</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm assuming you meant &quot;feed&quot;, so I fixed that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I said I would get you back</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is this supposed to say &quot;I said I would get back to you&quot;?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Alright, so let me just start by saying I think you have a pretty well put together article here. It's written pretty well, dialogue actually sounds like something a real person would say, and all the notes and observations feel natural and tonally sound.
<p>That being said: I'm not a huge fan of the actual anomaly. It's a skin condition that helps sad people be happy, and seems to have a vaguely malevolent way of propagating. Normally I'd suggest trying to build a story around your scip, but you have done that pretty well. I don't know, it's just not grabbing me. Tentative neutral vote for now, but I may come around upon reread</p>

